media: i2c: ov8865: only expose the native mode, the others stall

On a Surface Book 1 (and, judging by the shape of the bug, anywhere the
sensor is behind the Intel IPU3 / CIO2 bridge) only ov8865_modes[0], the
native 3264x2448 mode, streams continuously. The other three modes deliver
roughly one frame and then stop:

    mode        v4l2-ctl --stream-mmap --stream-count=5
    3264x2448   5/5 frames, rc=0
    3264x1836   0.99 frames, then nothing
    1632x1224   0.99 frames, then nothing
     800x600    1.00 frames, then nothing

Alternating good/bad/good/bad reproduces the same pattern, so it is a
property of the mode and not of stream-on state. dmesg shows

    ipu3-cio2: payload length is 2585088, received 2588672
    CSI-2 receiver port 0: frame sync error

- 3584 bytes more than expected, then desync. The timing tables
(HTS/VTS) for the non-native modes look wrong; the receiver overflows.

Why this is fatal in practice: libcamera picks the *smallest* mode that is
>= the requested size. An application asking for 1280x720 therefore gets
1632x1224, i.e. a broken mode. GNOME Snapshot asks for exactly 1280x720
(it cannot ask for more - the IPU3 pipeline caps every camera at
1280x720), so it gets nothing at all, and the never-written zero buffer
renders as pure green. YUV(0,0,0) is RGB(0,154,0).

The proper fix needs the OV8865 datasheet: ov8865_pll1_config has a single
configuration shared by all modes while ov8865_pll2_config has per-mode
variants, and ov8865_mode_pll1_rate()/_configure() take a mode pointer and
then ignore it, so PLL1 - and therefore the MIPI clock, pll1_rate / m_div
/ 2 - is reported as the same 360 MHz for every mode. Without binning PLL1
numbers there is nothing to correct.

As a workaround, hide the broken modes: VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES reports
only the native one, so libcamera has no choice, and set_fmt pins the
mode as well so that a caller driving V4L2 directly cannot select a
broken one either.

Cost: the sensor always runs 3264x2448 and the IMGU downsamples (2.55x for
a 1280x720 request). Field of view gets *wider*, not narrower - the
non-native modes crop, the native one uses the full array.

Applies to : linux v6.19.x, drivers/media/i2c/ov8865.c
Apply with : patch -p1 < 0003-...patch     (run from the kernel tree root)

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/ov8865.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/ov8865.c
@@ -2688,6 +2688,17 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Surface Book 1 workaround: only ov8865_modes[0] (3264x2448 native) streams
+ * reliably on this sensor/ACPI combination -- the other three modes deliver
+ * roughly one frame and then stall, which libcamera surfaces to applications
+ * as a never-filled (green) buffer. GNOME Snapshot is hit hardest: it asks for
+ * 1280x720, libcamera picks the smallest mode >= that request (1632x1224) and
+ * gets nothing. Hide the broken modes so the native one is always selected,
+ * whatever resolution the application asks for.
+ */
+#define OV8865_NR_USABLE_MODES		1
+
 static int ov8865_set_fmt(struct v4l2_subdev *subdev,
 			  struct v4l2_subdev_state *sd_state,
 			  struct v4l2_subdev_format *format)
@@ -2720,14 +2731,8 @@
 	if (!mbus_code)
 		mbus_code = ov8865_mbus_codes[0];
 
-	/* Find the mode with nearest dimensions. */
-	mode = v4l2_find_nearest_size(ov8865_modes, ARRAY_SIZE(ov8865_modes),
-				      output_size_x, output_size_y,
-				      mbus_format->width, mbus_format->height);
-	if (!mode) {
-		ret = -EINVAL;
-		goto complete;
-	}
+	/* Pin the request to the only mode that streams -- see above. */
+	mode = &ov8865_modes[0];
 
 	ov8865_mbus_format_fill(mbus_format, mbus_code, mode);
 
@@ -2765,7 +2770,7 @@
 {
 	const struct ov8865_mode *mode;
 
-	if (size_enum->index >= ARRAY_SIZE(ov8865_modes))
+	if (size_enum->index >= OV8865_NR_USABLE_MODES)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	mode = &ov8865_modes[size_enum->index];
